# One test, two views

A TypeScript case is visible as its own entry in the VS Code node:test tree and as its own row in the GitHub check. Adding or renaming a test updates both, because both read the same `test()` calls.

## How the two views stay the same

- Glue specs: `typescript/*.test.ts`, emitted to `typescript/dist`, run by `scripts/run-ts-glue-test-suite.mjs`.
- Sample client/server specs: `samples/ts-microservice/node-client/src/microservice.integration.test.ts`, emitted to `dist/microservice.integration.test.js`, run by `scripts/run-ts-microservice-tests.mjs`. The emitted name ends in `.test.js`, which is the only name the VS Code node:test explorer lists.
- The editor includes those emitted files (`.vscode/settings.json`). CI runs the same scripts and publishes their junit files, together with `ctest --output-junit`, as one GitHub check named Tests. Each CTest case is a row in that same check.
- Do not register these files as CTest entries. A CTest row is one entry for the whole file.

## When you add or change a test

- Call `test("exact name", …)` with a string literal. A name built in a loop is one explorer node and several runtime cases.
- JSON and BER each get their own literal, for example `test("… (JSON)", …)` and `test("… (BER)", …)`.
- Name the file `*.test.ts` so the emit is `*.test.js`. The explorer ignores other names, including `run-tests.js`.
- Put the file under `typescript/` or `samples/ts-microservice/node-client/src/`. A new directory must be added to `nodejs-testing.include` and to the CI junit publish path in the same change.
